# 0. Rerun mandate

This document reruns the **same core product-pressure scenario, same original Shot cases, same original adversarial negatives, and same cross-sequence invariants** defined by SoloRing Full-Sequence Product Pressure Test v1.0, preserves the explicitly labeled **v1.4 dialogue/performance extension case** and **v1.5 editorial/finishing preservation extension case**, and adds one explicitly labeled **v1.6 deterministic editorial-resolution/materialization extension case**. The additive v1.6 case tests that accepted editorial meaning becomes deterministic before execution, preview/final paths cannot reinterpret it independently, and preservation conform versus composition rendering remains an execution strategy rather than editorial authority.

The original pressure-test specification is not edited to make the revised architecture pass. Its exact SHA-256 remains:

```text
d25116eb0d61eaac2012e15872bf6e22c45c0644466669af3c913ca65cfec996
```

Only the architecture under test changes:

```text
Product & Production Architecture v1.6
        ↓
all v1.1 authority corrections preserved
        ↓
v1.4 dialogue/performance authority preserved
        ↓
v1.5 editorial/finishing preservation perimeter preserved
        ↓
v1.6 deterministic editorial resolution + materialization-mode separation integrated
        ↓
Product & Production Architecture v1.5
```

The rerun asks one regression question:

> **Does v1.6 still support the unchanged full-sequence product test without authority ambiguity, identity loss, temporal contradiction, hidden current-state dependence, or model/executor memory becoming production truth — while preserving the v1.4 dialogue/performance correction, the v1.5 source/proxy/preservation perimeter, and preventing unresolved defaults or materialization strategy from becoming editorial truth?**

The unchanged core pressure-test scenario does **not** independently prove every v1.3 construction principle, the v1.4 dialogue/performance contract, or a future complete editorial/finishing subsystem. In particular, capability-reuse experiments, shared-subset adapter equivalence, task-context compilation, mutable-discovery freezing, typed verdict APIs, delta-execution quality, profile-scoped qualification, property-scoped reference evidence, and attempt-lineage mechanics require their own future design/source gates. This rerun proves that adding those principles does not regress the already accepted production-world authority model.

Because qualification/state-variant/reference-role mistakes can create authority contradictions that the original scenario did not exercise, the preserved **v1.3 additive architecture stress appendix** remains. Version 1.4 adds the separate dialogue/lip-sync extension case in §13A. Version 1.5 adds the separate editorial/finishing preservation case in §13B and QT-7. Version 1.6 adds the separate deterministic editorial-resolution/materialization case in §13C and QT-8. These additions are architecture questions only; they do not alter the frozen v1.0 pressure-test specification or its hash.

A capability that is intentionally future work may remain unimplemented without failing this architecture test, provided the authority boundary, historical behavior, and failure semantics are coherent enough to assign ownership in the next capability map.

