# 22. Preserved v1.3 additive architecture stress appendix

These cases are additive architecture questions. They do not change the frozen v1.0 pressure-test specification.

## 22.1 QT-1 — Same Production Revision, different realization profiles

Scenario:

```text
Desk Revision 4 is published once.
Qualification under Profile A passes.
Qualification under Profile B fails or requires review.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- both qualification results coexist immutably;
- neither result mutates Desk Revision 4;
- current production policy may allow A and block B;
- changing profile applicability never rewrites old evidence.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** APR-114–115 and Architecture §10.7–10.10 define the required separation.

## 22.2 QT-2 — Two references govern different properties

Scenario:

```text
Reference A specifies face identity.
Reference B specifies wardrobe construction.
Reference C is an anti-reference forbidding one surface property.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- reference role is explicit and property-scoped;
- B does not silently override face identity;
- C remains an inspectable prohibition independent of renderer negative-prompt syntax;
- adopted claims constrain existing authority rather than becoming a new reference authority domain.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** APR-112–113 and Architecture §10.9/§17.11 close the ambiguity.

## 22.3 QT-3 — Forehead cut plus wet wardrobe without variant explosion

Scenario:

```text
Eva becomes wet.
Later a forehead cut appears.
Both conditions persist for several Shots.
Later Eva dries while the cut remains.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- `wet` and `forehead_cut` remain independent state dimensions on the same durable subject;
- a renderer-specific state realization may vary, but it does not create a new semantic identity for every combination;
- story-time transitions can change either fact independently.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Architecture §12.8 and APR-120 preserve composable state.

## 22.4 QT-4 — Recompile prompt without changing Shot truth

Scenario:

```text
Same captured Shot and production world.
Compiler/profile produces instruction artifact A.
A newer compatible compiler produces instruction artifact B.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- the authoritative Shot/world remains unchanged;
- instruction artifacts are derived and provenance-bearing;
- historical execution pins the applicable compiler/runtime identity;
- a prompt edit alone cannot rewrite Shot authority.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Architecture §16.6/§17.11 and APR-116 preserve the boundary.

## 22.5 QT-5 — Raw attempt exists but is not selected

Scenario:

```text
Generation attempt 7 produces a technically successful clip.
Review rejects it.
Attempt 8 is approved.
Editorial requests material for the Shot.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- attempt 7 remains immutable evidence but is not consumed by edit/finishing;
- attempt 8 is the approved/select path;
- raw directory ordering or newest-file selection cannot decide canon.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Existing Take approval plus Architecture §18.9/APR-119 preserves the selection boundary.

## 22.6 QT-6 — Controlled iteration preserves attempt history

Scenario:

```text
Attempt 12 uses realization input set X.
Attempt 13 intentionally changes one declared input.
Attempt 14 intentionally changes two declared inputs.
```

Required architecture behavior:

- each attempt identifies its parent and exact delta;
- all prior attempts remain inspectable;
- single-variable iteration is preferred but not falsified as a universal requirement;
- attempt delta is execution evidence, not production-world authority.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Architecture §18.8/APR-118 defines the lineage rule.

---

## 22.7 QT-7 — Approved Take trim/reorder preserves source and proxy boundaries

Scenario:

```text
approved Take A → retained source S-A
approved Take B → retained source S-B
proxy P-A generated for playback
edit uses exact interval A1 from S-A, then exact interval B1 from S-B
final conform uses a different executor than the preview path
```

Required architecture behavior:

- the edit continues to resolve S-A/S-B even if P-A is regenerated or unavailable;
- Take A and Take B remain immutable;
- executor-specific commands may change without redefining the edit;
- the conform records exact source/output lineage and the strongest preservation class actually achieved;
- `STREAM_EXACT`/`BOUNDARY_REENCODE`/`FULL_REENCODE` never substitute for production-property review.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Architecture §18.10 and APR-121–124 establish the required perimeter without prematurely freezing the future editorial schema.

---

## 22.8 QT-8 — Preview and final materialization cannot independently resolve editorial meaning

Scenario:

```text
approved Takes A/B
authoring candidate leaves transition choice to a convenience default
editor accepts one resolved transition + exact duration
preview uses executor P
final uses executor F with a mixed preservation/composition strategy
```

Required architecture behavior:

- acceptance captures the resolved transition identity/duration rather than preserving an unresolved random/default instruction;
- both executors consume the same resolved source intervals, order, transition meaning, graphics/audio placement, and time mapping;
- P and F may differ in quality or media strategy without changing editorial meaning;
- final materialization records which intervals were preserved and which were newly rendered;
- a backend that cannot satisfy the resolved edit fails rather than inventing a different interpretation.

**Verdict: PASS at architecture-contract level.** Architecture v1.6 §18.10.5–.7 and APR-125–126 define the required separation without freezing the future editorial schema.

